  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 316 AM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nSaturday night-Monday...A shortwave trough that has been \ncamping across the western CONUS for most of the week will \nfinally propagate eastward into the Northern Rockies Saturday \nnight. Shortwave ridging over the Mid-to-Upper Mississippi River\nValley introduces southwesterly flow aloft, pulling another \nmid-level vorticity lobe northward into the north-central \nPlains. This mid-level trough is forecasted to redevelop storms \n(50-70% coverage) along a cold front moving through the area. \nThe SPC has a Marginal Risk, Level 1 out of 5, for most of the \noutlook area for isolated damaging winds, but the latest LREF \nguidance only has a 20-40% joint probability of SBCAPE exceeding\n500 J/kg and the sfc-to-500 mb bulk shear exceeding 30 knots. \nTherefore, any convection that forms should struggle to \norganize.\n\nAfter Saturday night, scattered PoPs (40-70%) will stick around\ninto Monday as weak shortwaves rotating around the main trough \naxis, now over the Northern Plains, keeps CVA over the area. By \nMonday though, most precipitation will be in the form of rain \nshowers as the boundary and low pressure over Missouri drifts \nfurther south and northeasterly flow from a high pressure over \nOntario pumps in cooler air.\n\nTuesday-Wednesday...The shortwave trough over the Northern \nPlains finally reaches the Midwest on Tuesday. Isolated cold- \ncore showers (20-30% coverage) could stick around during the day\nbefore subsidence sets in on the backside of the trough heading\ninto Tuesday night and Wednesday. The persistent northeasterly \nflow should allow for seasonable temperatures with highs only in\nthe mid-60s to low 70s (60-90% chance)!",
